# Cursor Rules

## Component Creation Rules

### When Adding Components

**CRITICAL: Storybook stories are NOT required when adding components**

- ❌ DO NOT create Storybook stories when adding new components
- ❌ DO NOT create `.stories.tsx` or `.stories.vue` files
- ✅ Focus on component functionality and implementation
- ✅ Only create Storybook stories if explicitly requested by the user

### Component Structure

When creating new components:

1. **UI primitives** (`src/components/ui/`)
   - Base **shadcn-vue / Reka UI** components (CLI output; see root `components.json` alias `"ui": "@/components/ui"`)
   - Use `pnpm dlx shadcn-vue@latest add <component>` to install — files land under `src/components/ui/<name>/`
   - In Atomic Design terms this layer is the **“atoms”** folder; **this repo uses `ui/`, not `atoms/`**
   - No Storybook required

2. **Molecules** (`src/components/molecules/`)
   - Complex components composed from **`@/components/ui/*`**
   - Custom business logic and compositions
   - No Storybook required unless explicitly requested

3. **Views / feature modules** (`src/views/` and `src/views/{module}/`)
   - Routed pages and module-specific screens (list, detail, module form modals)
   - Prefer **`src/views/{module}/`** for feature-specific modals and pages (see **`.agents/rules/structure.md`**)
   - No Storybook required

### General Component Guidelines

- Use TypeScript for all new files
- Follow Vue 3 Composition API patterns
- Use proper typing, avoid `any`
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling
- Use `cn()` utility from `@/lib/utils` for conditional classes
- Follow shadcn-vue styling patterns
- Use proper component layering (**`ui` primitives → molecules → views** for pages; generic Atomic Design docs may say “atoms” or “containers” — **atoms = `src/components/ui/`**, **containers** map to **`src/views/{module}/`**)

### Package Management

- **ALWAYS use `pnpm`** as the package manager
- Never use `npm` or `yarn`
- When installing dependencies: `pnpm add <package>`
- When installing dev dependencies: `pnpm add -D <package>`

### shadcn-vue Component Installation

**CRITICAL: Always use the CLI, never generate code manually**

```bash
# Install a shadcn-vue component
pnpm dlx shadcn-vue@latest add <component-name>
```

### Custom Styles & CSS Classes

**When using custom styles:**

- ✅ **ALWAYS check `src/style.css` first** before creating inline Tailwind classes with arbitrary values (e.g., `w-[365px]`, `h-[40px]`)
- ✅ If a style already exists in `style.css`, use the existing class
- ✅ If a style doesn't exist but is reusable/common, add it to `style.css` in the `@layer utilities` section
- ✅ Only use inline Tailwind arbitrary values for truly one-off, component-specific styles
- ✅ Common reusable styles (like filter input dimensions, common button sizes, etc.) should be in `style.css`

Example:

```css
/* In style.css */
.filter-input {
  width: 365px;
  height: 40px;
}
```

```vue
<!-- In component -->
<Input class="filter-input bg-white border border-gray-300" />
```

### Code Quality & Linting

**After completing ANY task, ALWAYS:**

1. Run lint and surface issues (see output):

```bash
pnpm lint
```

2. **Report** lint/type problems to the user; **do not** apply bulk auto-fixes unless the user asks to fix them in the same task.
3. If you use `pnpm lint --fix`, do so **only** when the user explicitly wants fixes applied — not by default after every task.

### DO NOT

- ❌ Create Storybook stories when adding components
- ❌ Manually create shadcn-vue component code
- ❌ Use npm or yarn
- ❌ Auto-fix lint issues without the user asking
- ❌ Skip the linting step
- ❌ Use `any` type excessively
- ❌ Create documentation after completing tasks (unless explicitly requested)
- ❌ Hardcode text directly in code (use i18n if available)

### Dialog Component Guidelines

**When using Dialog components:**

- ✅ Use `DialogContent` from **`@/components/ui/dialog`** for all dialogs (install with `pnpm dlx shadcn-vue@latest add dialog` if the module is not present yet)
- ✅ Control click outside behavior using `closeOnClickOutside` prop:
  - Set `closeOnClickOutside={false}` to prevent dialog from closing when clicking outside
  - Default is `true` (dialog closes on outside click)
- ✅ Control ESC key behavior using `closeOnEscape` prop:
  - Set `closeOnEscape={false}` to prevent dialog from closing when pressing ESC key
  - Default is `true` (dialog closes on ESC key)
- ✅ Always handle dialog state properly:
  - Use `v-model` or `:open` and `@update:open` for controlled dialogs
  - Clear form data and validation when dialog closes (in `handleOpenChange` or watcher)
- ✅ Use `showCloseButton` prop to control close button visibility (default: `true`)

Example:

```vue
<DialogContent :close-on-click-outside="false" :close-on-escape="false" :show-close-button="true">
  <!-- Dialog content -->
</DialogContent>
```

### ConfirmDialog Component Guidelines

**When using ConfirmDialog for delete confirmations:**

- ✅ Use `ConfirmDialog` from `@/components/molecules/confirm-dialog` for delete confirmations
- ✅ Follow the standard **ConfirmDialog** pattern (entity ID + name in the message, Japanese button labels); reuse the same structure across list screens:
  ```vue
  <ConfirmDialog
    v-model="isDeleteDialogOpen"
    title="削除確認"
    :message="`{Entity}を削除します。よろしいですか。\n「{Entity}ID : ${selectedItem?.id}」\n「{Entity}名: ${selectedItem?.name}」`"
    confirm-text="はい"
    cancel-text="いいえ"
    variant="destructive"
    :loading="isDeleting"
    :show-cancel-icon="true"
    :show-confirm-icon="true"
    @confirm="handleDeleteConfirm"
    @cancel="handleDeleteCancel"
  />
  ```
- ✅ In `handleDeleteConfirm`:
  - Call mutation hook
  - Show success toast (in view only; do not show toast inside mutation)
  - Close dialog and clear selected item
  - Do **not** call `refetch()`; list refetch is handled by mutation `onSuccess` via `queryClient.invalidateQueries`
- ✅ Always include entity ID and name in the confirmation message
- ✅ Use `はい` for confirm text and `いいえ` for cancel text (Japanese standard)

### Form & CRUD Behavior Guidelines (Common for all screens)

**When implementing create/edit/delete flows with dialogs:**

- ✅ **Success behavior (common rule)**:
  - On **create / edit / delete success**, always (in the **view component**):
    - Show a **success toast** (use `useSuccessToast` composable)
    - **Close the dialog / confirm modal** and clear any selected item
  - **Do not** call `refetch()` in the view; mutation hooks should use `queryClient.invalidateQueries` in `onSuccess` so the list refetches automatically
  - **Do not** show success toast inside mutation hooks; handle toast only in the view (e.g. in `handleFormSuccess`, `handleDeleteConfirm`)
- ✅ **Error behavior (common rule)**:
  - On **create / edit / delete failure**:
    - Rely on the **global HTTP client interceptors** to show **error toasts**
    - Keep **form dialogs open** so the user can fix input and retry
    - For **delete**, close the confirm dialog after the mutation finishes (success or error) and clear any selected item
- ✅ **Validation behavior** (align with **common-rule-01**):
  - Keep the **primary Submit** button **enabled** whenever the form is not submitting (including **edit** with **no** field changes, and when the form is **invalid**).
  - On **Submit** click, validate the **entire** form and show errors for invalid fields.
  - Validate on **change** and show inline errors under the related field.
  - When the value becomes valid, clear that field’s error.
  - Use `isFormValid` only inside submit handlers / to decide whether to call the API — **not** to disable the submit button.
- ✅ **Dialog close behavior**:
  - Do **not** allow closing form dialogs via **ESC key** or **click outside**:
    - Always set `:close-on-click-outside="false"` and `:close-on-escape="false"` on `DialogContent` for create/edit forms
  - When a dialog is closed (via cancel or success):
    - **Clear form values and validation state** in a dedicated `resetForm()` helper
    - This applies both to create and edit modes
- ✅ **Edit mode submit** (common-rule-01):
  - Do **not** disable submit when the user has made **no** changes in edit mode; **Submit** stays enabled (except while `isSubmitting`).
  - You may still use `hasFormChanges` for **discard-on-close** or **unsaved** warnings only — not for disabling the primary submit button.
- ✅ **Error visibility**:
  - Validation errors should be **shown inline under inputs** when:
    - The field has been blurred at least once (touched), **or**
    - The user attempted **Submit** and the field is invalid (full-form validation on submit)

### TanStack Form & shared validators

**When implementing forms (search forms, create/edit modals):**

- ✅ Use **TanStack Form** (`useForm` from `@tanstack/vue-form`) for form state; do not use `reactive` + `toRefs` for form state.
- ✅ Use validators from the **shared file** `src/lib/validators`. If a validation rule you need does **not** exist there, **add it to `src/lib/validators/index.ts` first**, then use it in the component. Do **not** define validation schema inline in the component when an equivalent can live in the shared file.
- ✅ Reference: See `docs/examples/tanstack-form-example.vue` for a minimal form using TanStack Form + shared validators.

### Table Action Column Guidelines (Common for all list pages)

**When implementing action columns (Edit/Delete) in tables:**

- ✅ **Column layout**:
  - Name the column header `操作`
  - Center-align the column (`align: 'center'` or `justify-center`)
  - Use a container like `class="flex items-center justify-center gap-2"` inside the cell
- ✅ **Buttons & labels**:
  - Use **`Button`** from `@/components/ui/button` for actions with clear Japanese labels:
    - **Edit**: label `編集` with pencil icon (`Pencil` or `Edit`)
    - **Delete**: label `削除` with trash icon (`Trash2`)
  - Keep spacing, icon size, and colors consistent across management tables (see **`TableDemo.vue`** in this repo for a minimal table + button example)
- ✅ **Interaction behavior**:
  - Always stop row click propagation for action buttons:
    - `onClick: (e: Event) => { e.stopPropagation(); handleEdit(row) }`
  - Keep actions **visible on all rows** (no hidden-on-hover patterns)
- ✅ **Reusability**:
  - Reuse the same action column pattern across:
    - Store management
    - Expense category management
    - Category management
    - Assessment method management
  - When adding a new management screen, **start from an existing action-column implementation** and adapt only the entity-specific handlers

### API Create/Update Guidelines

- ✅ **Core API (create/update)** — same rules as reads:
  - Methods in `src/apis/cores/*-api.ts` call the backend and map **DTO → domain model** in the **core** layer (see **`.agents/rules/data-model.md`** and **`api-rule.md`**).
  - **Service hooks** (`src/apis/services/**`) unwrap `response.data`, treat values as **models**, and expose them to views/stores — **no** DTO mapping inside components or inside hooks beyond unwrapping.

### Disabled Input Style Guidelines (Common for ID/read-only fields)

**When displaying read-only fields (IDs, system-controlled values) in dialogs:**

- ✅ Use **`Input`** from `@/components/ui/input` with:
  - `disabled` attribute set
  - `class="bg-neutral-100"` to match the standard disabled background used in store edit screens
- ✅ Typical usage:
  - Show IDs (e.g. `店舗ID`, `経費分類ID`, `査定方法ID`) in **edit dialogs only**
  - Do **not** show these fields in create mode
- ✅ Do **not** introduce custom one-off disabled styles:
  - If a different disabled style is truly needed, define a **reusable utility class** in `src/style.css` under `@layer utilities` and reference it consistently

## Unit Testing Rules

**Stack:**

- Test runner: **Vitest** (`pnpm test`, `pnpm test:watch`, `pnpm test:ui`, `pnpm test:coverage`)
- Test location: `tests/unit/**` (mirrors `src/**` by domain)
- HTTP mocking: **MSW** (see `tests/setup.ts` and MSW guides if added later)

**Coverage Targets (overall direction):**

- **Business logic (apis, models, stores, validators, utilities)**: **90–95%+**
- **Complex views/components (forms, tables with actions)**: **80–90%+** on important paths
- Simple presentational components: lower priority; test only when they contain logic.

**When adding or changing code, prefer: test‑first or test‑along.**

### What must have tests

- API cores in `src/apis/cores/**` (request params, mapping, error handling)
- API services/hooks in `src/apis/services/**` (loading/success/error states)
- Models (`src/models/**`) with `fromApi`/`fromApiList` or computed getters
- Shared validators and utilities (`src/lib/**`, `src/lib/validators/**`)
- Stores with business logic (`src/stores/**`)
- Views/components that contain **non‑trivial logic** (forms, CRUD flows, branching UI)

### Test placement & naming

- Place test files in `tests/unit/...` mirroring `src/...` structure.
  - Example: `src/apis/cores/expense-api.ts` → `tests/unit/apis/cores/expense-api.test.ts`
- Name tests: `{name}.test.ts` / `{name}.test.tsx` / `{name}.test.vue` depending on SFC vs TS.
- Test names: `should [behavior] when [condition]`.

### Patterns & good practices

- ✅ Use **AAA pattern** (Arrange–Act–Assert) with clear comments in complex tests.
- ✅ Prefer **behavioral assertions** (what user sees/what function returns), not implementation details.
- ✅ Use **MSW** for HTTP where possible instead of manually mocking fetch/axios in every test.
- ✅ Use `vi.mock` for internal modules (stores, composables, utilities) when isolating units.
- ✅ Keep one logical scenario per test; avoid if/else inside tests.
- ✅ Use `test.each` for the same logic with multiple inputs instead of copy‑pasting tests.
- ❌ Do **not** snapshot‑test large components by default; prefer specific assertions.
- ❌ Do **not** test third‑party library internals (React Query, TanStack Form, etc.).

### Minimum coverage per unit (guideline)

For each important unit (API core/service, model, store, complex view/component):

- [ ] Happy path (normal success flow)
- [ ] Error paths (API error, thrown exception, invalid input)
- [ ] Edge cases (boundary values, empty lists, null/undefined paths)
- [ ] State variants (loading / error / empty) where applicable
- [ ] Conditional branches (if/else, early returns)

### Running tests before delivery

Before considering a task "done":

- ✅ Run `pnpm test:run` for the affected domain/tests.
- ✅ Run `pnpm test:coverage` and quickly inspect:
  - New/changed files have coverage aligned with the goals above.
  - Any intentionally untested branches are documented in code comments or PR description.

### DO

- ✅ Use pnpm for all package operations
- ✅ Use shadcn-vue CLI for component installation
- ✅ Run `pnpm lint` (or `pnpm lint --fix` when the user wants fixes applied) after substantive edits
- ✅ Report lint errors without fixing
- ✅ Follow TypeScript best practices
- ✅ Use proper component layering
- ✅ Write clean, readable code
- ✅ Use meaningful variable names
- ✅ Add proper TypeScript types
- ✅ Control dialog click outside behavior using `closeOnClickOutside` prop
- ✅ Follow ConfirmDialog pattern for delete confirmations (see above)
